WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:04.990
- You are what you are,
nothing more and nothing less.

00:00:04.990 --> 00:00:07.730
Be proud of who you are, no matter,

00:00:07.730 --> 00:00:10.747
God made you so you are what you are

00:00:10.747 --> 00:00:12.673
and you ain't what you ain't.

00:00:14.710 --> 00:00:16.523
See, I was raised in a holler

00:00:16.523 --> 00:00:20.750
that didn't even get a
road till like the 50s

00:00:23.170 --> 00:00:26.550
and the food we got, we had to raise it.

00:00:26.550 --> 00:00:27.383
That's what I was telling them.

00:00:27.383 --> 00:00:31.300
When I was four year old
or five, a little kid,

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:35.450
they made a hoe just for our size

00:00:35.450 --> 00:00:40.450
to hoe taters in the gardens.

00:00:40.610 --> 00:00:41.770
You know what I'm saying?

00:00:41.770 --> 00:00:44.770
That's all we got to do
was work in the garden.

00:00:44.770 --> 00:00:48.560
And I mean, well, we played
ball and stuff like that,

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:51.117
but we was like...

00:00:52.310 --> 00:00:53.780
I was raised in the mountain.

00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:55.690
I mean, literally in the mountain.

00:00:55.690 --> 00:00:57.650
You know what I'm saying?

00:00:57.650 --> 00:01:00.430
I'd love to take you
to where I was raised.

00:01:00.430 --> 00:01:01.500
There's a big water fall.

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:03.760
Oh, it's beautiful.

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:05.360
I mean, it is, it's like...

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:08.200
It ain't like the Cumberland Falls,

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:10.070
but it's probably more...

00:01:10.070 --> 00:01:14.250
It's even prettier than
that I guess it is.

00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:15.460
- But what was it like growing up

00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:16.293
in the mountains like that?

00:01:16.293 --> 00:01:17.880
You're isolated from
everybody else, right?

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:21.020
- Yeah, but we never got bored.

00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:22.130
We just...

00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:26.080
We would build us a sled
like in the winter time

00:01:28.533 --> 00:01:33.057
and stay out in the snow
and ride sleds till dark.

00:01:33.057 --> 00:01:34.480
And we played in the creeks.

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:38.950
We'd dam the creeks up or go
to the falls and swim in it.

00:01:38.950 --> 00:01:43.950
You know? I mean, we
always had something to do

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:47.230
even though we had nothing.

00:01:47.230 --> 00:01:49.730
Or build like a wooden wagons

00:01:51.190 --> 00:01:54.510
and we pretty much didn't get toys

00:01:54.510 --> 00:01:56.963
till we were 10; 10 year old.

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:00.890
I believe when I got my first bicycle and

00:02:03.012 --> 00:02:05.020
life was hard, but it was good.

00:02:05.020 --> 00:02:06.699
- It's a great way for
a kid to grow up, right.

00:02:06.699 --> 00:02:07.532
- Right.

00:02:09.290 --> 00:02:11.130
- You wouldn't change it, right?

00:02:11.130 --> 00:02:12.413
- Oh, no, no.

00:02:15.070 --> 00:02:17.083
I loved how I was raised really.

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:20.780
- Tell me about your parents.

00:02:20.780 --> 00:02:23.420
You had both your parents growing up?

00:02:23.420 --> 00:02:27.698
- Well, my mom and then she
went to Baltimore when I was 12

00:02:27.698 --> 00:02:30.053
and I stayed with my
grandma and my grandpa.

00:02:31.150 --> 00:02:36.150
And he was like the best
person in the whole world.

00:02:41.731 --> 00:02:42.564
- And how much...

00:02:42.564 --> 00:02:44.030
How far did you go in school?

00:02:44.030 --> 00:02:45.723
- Till my junior year.

00:02:48.570 --> 00:02:51.720
That was a bad mistake
dropping out of school though.

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:53.188
It really was.

00:02:53.188 --> 00:02:55.185
- Is that your biggest regret?

00:02:55.185 --> 00:02:57.852
- Yeah, it is my biggest regret.

00:03:00.351 --> 00:03:02.020
'Cause I played basketball.

00:03:02.020 --> 00:03:03.093
I was like...

00:03:03.093 --> 00:03:05.123
I was a real good ballplayer.

00:03:06.708 --> 00:03:08.700
If I had stayed in school

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:09.700
I probably...

00:03:09.700 --> 00:03:11.260
I might not even...

00:03:11.260 --> 00:03:14.900
Well, I ain't gonna say I'd
probably move, but you know,

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:16.250
I would've had a different life.

00:03:16.250 --> 00:03:17.500
You know what I'm saying?

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:21.400
And I left and went to Baltimore

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:25.070
and got there and stayed like
three months and got homesick.

00:03:25.070 --> 00:03:29.160
And back to the mountains I come and I...

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:30.943
- I've heard that story a lot
from a lot of people here.

00:03:30.943 --> 00:03:32.151
They leave and they come back.

00:03:32.151 --> 00:03:32.984
- Yeah, that's what I was telling him.

00:03:32.984 --> 00:03:36.250
I got to the bus station at
two o'clock in the morning

00:03:36.250 --> 00:03:39.070
and the bus was full.

00:03:39.070 --> 00:03:43.320
So he said, "the next
bus is tomorrow at two."

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:45.600
And I just broke down and started crying.

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:47.500
I said "Please man. Let me on this bus

00:03:48.839 --> 00:03:51.423
'cause I wanna go home.

00:03:51.423 --> 00:03:52.823
I wanna come see my pappaw."

00:03:53.710 --> 00:03:55.580
And he said, "well, you
can sit in the floor

00:03:55.580 --> 00:03:56.607
or on the steps."

00:03:57.630 --> 00:04:01.600
And he made me a little seat
beside his and I rode...

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:05.043
It was a five-hour drive from
Baltimore to Pennsylvania,

00:04:08.805 --> 00:04:10.940
but I'd of walked just to get back home.

00:04:10.940 --> 00:04:12.890
You know what I'm saying?

00:04:12.890 --> 00:04:13.997
- Oh yeah.

00:04:13.997 --> 00:04:17.080
- Yeah. We would go swimming everyday

00:04:18.193 --> 00:04:21.403
like up Greasy and up to Bee Rock.

00:04:23.767 --> 00:04:26.640
And we just always had fun

00:04:28.941 --> 00:04:31.680
but everybody was real
poor where we was raised

00:04:33.790 --> 00:04:36.650
as a lot of them was alcoholics.

00:04:36.650 --> 00:04:39.050
A lot of times, you couldn't
even get up the holler for 'em

00:04:39.050 --> 00:04:41.850
'cause you had passed
five or six in the road

00:04:41.850 --> 00:04:44.400
passed out on the way up the holler.

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:45.290
You know what I'm saying?

00:04:45.290 --> 00:04:48.120
You'd have to drag them
out of the road to get by

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:51.160
and then half of them
would want to fight you.

00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:54.703
But all them old people
are dead now though.

00:04:55.840 --> 00:04:57.120
- You're the cousin of Geraldine

00:04:57.120 --> 00:04:58.075
who I interviewed a month ago.

00:04:58.075 --> 00:04:59.605
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:04:59.605 --> 00:05:03.373
My mammaw that raised me
and her mommy are sisters.

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:06.260
I love Geraldine.

00:05:06.260 --> 00:05:08.010
- Yeah. It's a rough life here.

00:05:08.010 --> 00:05:08.890
- Yeah.

00:05:08.890 --> 00:05:10.040
- But that struggle seems to make...

00:05:10.040 --> 00:05:14.240
- Yes, see she raised up five
boys pretty much on her own

00:05:15.750 --> 00:05:19.210
and I raised three pretty much on my own.

00:05:19.210 --> 00:05:21.170
- Where was the father?

00:05:21.170 --> 00:05:24.750
Well, he's in and out, but you know...

00:05:24.750 --> 00:05:27.450
I didn't want him around too much.

00:05:27.450 --> 00:05:31.160
- Yeah. How far did you go in school?

00:05:31.160 --> 00:05:32.603
- Till my junior year.

00:05:33.510 --> 00:05:35.450
I lacked one year of graduating.

00:05:38.975 --> 00:05:41.520
- And you raised three kids?

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:42.963
- Yeah. Three boys.

00:05:44.615 --> 00:05:45.973
- And how'd you support yourself?

00:05:47.730 --> 00:05:50.780
- I'd work for my uncle, my aunts

00:05:50.780 --> 00:05:53.340
just mostly little odd jobs

00:05:53.340 --> 00:05:56.574
or go in the mountain and dig roots up.

00:05:56.574 --> 00:05:57.604
Dig ginseng.

00:05:57.604 --> 00:05:59.350
- So you'd consider
yourself a mountain woman.

00:05:59.350 --> 00:06:00.980
- Yeah. Hell yeah.

00:06:00.980 --> 00:06:02.480
- There's a lot of mountain men,

00:06:02.480 --> 00:06:03.620
but you're a mountain woman.

00:06:03.620 --> 00:06:06.320
- I love the mountains. I
was raised in the mountains.

00:06:07.420 --> 00:06:10.210
I mean, I go ginsenging.

00:06:10.210 --> 00:06:12.470
Well anymore, I'm sort of...

00:06:12.470 --> 00:06:16.280
My feet and my heart's bad.

00:06:16.280 --> 00:06:19.090
I can't walk like I used to, but yeah,

00:06:19.090 --> 00:06:22.580
I'd go sometimes dig
three pounds of 'seng.

00:06:22.580 --> 00:06:25.140
what's the most I ever dug
at one time was three pounds.

00:06:25.140 --> 00:06:26.180
- Of ginseng?

00:06:26.180 --> 00:06:28.850
- Yeah. But every day...

00:06:28.850 --> 00:06:29.950
Good money in ginseng

00:06:31.100 --> 00:06:34.960
and we'd save it back, like
to live through the winter on.

00:06:34.960 --> 00:06:36.210
You know what I'm saying?

00:06:37.240 --> 00:06:40.417
Dig you out a root and
like a (indistinct).

00:06:40.417 --> 00:06:42.670
- And that's certainly
gotta be hard work, right?

00:06:42.670 --> 00:06:44.777
- Yeah, it's hard.

00:06:44.777 --> 00:06:45.610
- Yeah.

00:06:45.610 --> 00:06:48.913
- Yeah. I was ginsenging
up Double Creek one time.

00:06:50.629 --> 00:06:51.515
And I seen this...

00:06:51.515 --> 00:06:53.230
I thought was a big (indistinct)

00:06:53.230 --> 00:06:55.390
I got over there

00:06:55.390 --> 00:06:59.010
and there's a copperhead
wrapped around my left leg

00:06:59.010 --> 00:07:02.250
and I went to kick it off and when I did,

00:07:02.250 --> 00:07:04.310
I jumped and when I jumped

00:07:04.310 --> 00:07:08.330
I had leggins on up to
my knees, but I fell.

00:07:08.330 --> 00:07:09.900
And when I come up,

00:07:09.900 --> 00:07:12.400
I said, "Oh man! I
believe that snake bit me"

00:07:12.400 --> 00:07:15.770
and it bit me right an
inch above my leggins.

00:07:15.770 --> 00:07:20.770
So, and I ginsenged like
for three hours snake bit.

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:23.620
But when I got to the truck

00:07:23.620 --> 00:07:26.550
and took that leggin off,
my leg just blowed up.

00:07:26.550 --> 00:07:28.360
- You did it for how...

00:07:28.360 --> 00:07:29.193
Three hours?

00:07:29.193 --> 00:07:30.633
- Three hours after I got bit.

00:07:31.620 --> 00:07:32.510
- With a snake bite?

00:07:32.510 --> 00:07:33.880
- Yeah. But when I...

00:07:33.880 --> 00:07:35.447
Then it started hurting
real bad and I was like,

00:07:35.447 --> 00:07:38.410
"Oh. I ain't never going
to get out of this holler."

00:07:38.410 --> 00:07:39.440
- People die from that.

00:07:39.440 --> 00:07:42.960
- Oh yeah. I stayed in the
hospital I believe six days

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:46.050
but I didn't go to the ER
till like 11:00 that night.

00:07:46.050 --> 00:07:47.520
You know?

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:49.630
That's what made it bad, I guess,

00:07:49.630 --> 00:07:52.273
'cause I didn't go
straight to the hospital.

00:07:53.643 --> 00:07:55.480
Have you ever ginsenged?

00:07:55.480 --> 00:07:57.784
- I have never ginsenged.

00:07:57.784 --> 00:08:00.347
- You ought to go make
your show about that.

00:08:00.347 --> 00:08:02.930
(Sue laughing)

00:08:05.625 --> 00:08:07.300
- So what do you think of
the future of this region?

00:08:07.300 --> 00:08:08.700
It seems like the drugs have taken over

00:08:08.700 --> 00:08:09.740
the younger generation.

00:08:09.740 --> 00:08:11.840
- Yeah, it really has.

00:08:11.840 --> 00:08:16.080
I mean, people used to smoke pot.

00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:18.780
A lot of people thinks pot's real bad.

00:08:18.780 --> 00:08:20.090
I don't see it is.

00:08:20.090 --> 00:08:22.113
My uncle was an outlaw

00:08:22.113 --> 00:08:24.340
till he started smoking pot.

00:08:24.340 --> 00:08:25.870
When he started smoking pot

00:08:25.870 --> 00:08:30.070
he turned into a peaceful,
quiet, worked all the time...

00:08:30.070 --> 00:08:31.300
I mean he'd eat a lot

00:08:31.300 --> 00:08:33.710
and he'd got the munchies
and just chilled out.

00:08:33.710 --> 00:08:37.480
But then, when that
meth have come in here,

00:08:37.480 --> 00:08:39.200
it's destroyed the whole...

00:08:39.200 --> 00:08:41.950
It really has destroyed the whole area

00:08:41.950 --> 00:08:44.690
which it's everywhere you know,

00:08:44.690 --> 00:08:48.383
but I've lost so many friends to drugs.

00:08:51.700 --> 00:08:53.110
Everybody's on something.

00:08:53.110 --> 00:08:54.240
You know what I'm saying?

00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:57.313
- Yeah. Is that something
you struggled with as well?

00:08:58.600 --> 00:09:01.860
- Not now, but in past. Yeah, once,

00:09:01.860 --> 00:09:04.240
but it wasn't meth was mine.

00:09:04.240 --> 00:09:05.073
I got on...

00:09:05.073 --> 00:09:05.920
When my pappaw died

00:09:05.920 --> 00:09:08.840
I got on nerve pills real bad.

00:09:08.840 --> 00:09:10.000
And that was my...

00:09:12.420 --> 00:09:14.120
But I got off of them

00:09:14.120 --> 00:09:17.878
and my boy got killed three
year ago on a four wheeler.

00:09:17.878 --> 00:09:18.878
- I'm sorry.

00:09:20.627 --> 00:09:22.660
And they've tried to give me Xanax

00:09:23.770 --> 00:09:26.140
but I just won't go get 'em.

00:09:26.140 --> 00:09:26.973
'cause I know...

00:09:26.973 --> 00:09:27.900
You know what I'm saying?

00:09:27.900 --> 00:09:32.440
I don't wanna be back on them
'cause they're the best pill

00:09:32.440 --> 00:09:35.183
and they're the worst
pill that ever was made.

00:09:36.270 --> 00:09:39.570
But mostly around here
it's just meth or...

00:09:41.460 --> 00:09:44.660
Used to everybody drunk and got drunk

00:09:44.660 --> 00:09:48.523
and it was alcohol and maybe pot.

00:09:48.523 --> 00:09:52.690
Then, it went to meth and stuff like that,

00:09:52.690 --> 00:09:54.920
which heroin and stuff like that

00:09:54.920 --> 00:09:57.640
really ain't in this
part of the country bad.

00:09:57.640 --> 00:10:01.470
- What do you think of the
future for people here?

00:10:01.470 --> 00:10:02.303
'Cause drugs have taken over and...

00:10:02.303 --> 00:10:05.680
- Buddy, if they don't start
putting some kind of business

00:10:05.680 --> 00:10:10.680
like work here, the little
kids ain't got no hope

00:10:12.540 --> 00:10:14.150
in this part of the...

00:10:14.150 --> 00:10:18.750
They ain't 'cause a lot
of people don't know about

00:10:18.750 --> 00:10:21.373
how to make money in
the mountains and stuff.

00:10:21.373 --> 00:10:22.559
They just...

00:10:22.559 --> 00:10:23.392
There ain't no way of surviving, you know.

00:10:25.750 --> 00:10:27.598
What you gonna do?

00:10:27.598 --> 00:10:30.150
And they have to depend on a food stamp

00:10:30.150 --> 00:10:34.170
or government assistance 'cause...

00:10:34.170 --> 00:10:35.890
Or their kids would starve to death.

00:10:35.890 --> 00:10:37.810
You know what I mean?

00:10:37.810 --> 00:10:39.120
I was raised in this holler

00:10:39.120 --> 00:10:41.860
or it was up Trace
Branch I was telling you

00:10:41.860 --> 00:10:45.460
but up in there everybody
raised their own garden,

00:10:45.460 --> 00:10:49.420
but well...

00:10:50.470 --> 00:10:53.793
He was our neighbor,
but he was like afraid

00:10:56.250 --> 00:10:58.160
of the world or the law.

00:10:58.160 --> 00:11:00.423
So he built him a little tar shack.

00:11:01.479 --> 00:11:04.320
It had a little dirt floor
and all he had in there

00:11:04.320 --> 00:11:07.890
was a little bed and a stove.

00:11:07.890 --> 00:11:12.790
And I mean, he was happy with that.

00:11:12.790 --> 00:11:13.890
You know what I mean?

00:11:13.890 --> 00:11:15.410
You could build him a mansion

00:11:15.410 --> 00:11:17.310
and he wouldn't have lived in it.

00:11:17.310 --> 00:11:18.353
- A lot of people I've...

00:11:18.353 --> 00:11:20.320
- He was like...

00:11:20.320 --> 00:11:24.060
He loved his little tar shack I guess.

00:11:24.060 --> 00:11:25.880
- I've seen and I've met
a lot of people here now.

00:11:25.880 --> 00:11:28.400
And it seems like they live
a very, very simple life

00:11:28.400 --> 00:11:29.233
- Yes.

00:11:29.233 --> 00:11:30.250
- but they're also very happy.

00:11:30.250 --> 00:11:33.213
- Yeah. When we was growing
up, that's when FNS,

00:11:33.213 --> 00:11:36.400
like all these interns would come in

00:11:36.400 --> 00:11:38.607
and they would...

00:11:38.607 --> 00:11:40.920
All them nurses and
stuff would get together.

00:11:40.920 --> 00:11:45.310
My uncles and all them,
they had a bluegrass band.

00:11:45.310 --> 00:11:48.580
So they would play music
with our square dances

00:11:48.580 --> 00:11:52.760
like every Saturday night
and we made a lot of friends

00:11:52.760 --> 00:11:54.270
through that.

00:11:54.270 --> 00:11:57.690
Like one was Holly Cheever.

00:11:57.690 --> 00:12:00.573
She's a veterinarian now in New York.

00:12:01.600 --> 00:12:06.070
But we had, on Saturday nights

00:12:06.070 --> 00:12:07.680
you had that to look forward to.

00:12:07.680 --> 00:12:09.980
Then we started going to the wrestling.

00:12:09.980 --> 00:12:11.810
We would do that every weekend.

00:12:11.810 --> 00:12:15.310
And that was fun, but it was just...

00:12:15.310 --> 00:12:18.083
Life was sweet and simple, I guess.

00:12:21.420 --> 00:12:23.840
My grandma, my great-grandma right now,

00:12:23.840 --> 00:12:25.890
she's buried right in
the top of a mountain

00:12:25.890 --> 00:12:28.320
in an old hill road that used to,

00:12:28.320 --> 00:12:32.140
they used as a road, like, travel it.

00:12:32.140 --> 00:12:36.020
See, she would go around
and deliver babies.

00:12:36.020 --> 00:12:38.610
These old timers, when
they had their babies

00:12:38.610 --> 00:12:40.060
there weren't no nurses.

00:12:40.060 --> 00:12:42.260
That was for Mary Breckinridge.

00:12:42.260 --> 00:12:45.230
So, she was a granny
woman and they called her

00:12:45.230 --> 00:12:49.620
and pappaw said sometimes
four in the morning,

00:12:49.620 --> 00:12:52.010
somebody would come,
you're gonna have to come,

00:12:52.010 --> 00:12:56.430
let's see, so-and-so's in labor

00:12:56.430 --> 00:12:58.080
and she would get on her old mule

00:12:59.190 --> 00:13:01.204
and hit them old mountain roads

00:13:01.204 --> 00:13:05.160
that takes you from one holler to the next

00:13:05.160 --> 00:13:08.193
and deliver a baby, you know.

00:13:09.510 --> 00:13:13.080
That was probably, that
was my great grandma.

00:13:13.080 --> 00:13:17.170
And they was like the
first settlers really,

00:13:17.170 --> 00:13:18.833
like upstanding.

00:13:19.950 --> 00:13:22.280
Like Hugh Morgan, them ole Morgans.

00:13:22.280 --> 00:13:24.060
There was a bunch of them, but...

00:13:27.780 --> 00:13:31.030
Then, like in Trace Branch,
we didn't have a well,

00:13:31.030 --> 00:13:33.660
so we would...

00:13:33.660 --> 00:13:36.240
They'd take like a horse and a sled

00:13:36.240 --> 00:13:39.000
and my uncle Albert would go around,

00:13:39.000 --> 00:13:41.560
digging them dug wells for everybody,

00:13:41.560 --> 00:13:43.290
but we didn't have one.

00:13:43.290 --> 00:13:47.500
So, we'll go to the
neighbors and get water,

00:13:47.500 --> 00:13:49.990
fill these big cans up in the sled.

00:13:49.990 --> 00:13:52.670
And I remember riding in
the back of that sled,

00:13:52.670 --> 00:13:57.670
I loved that but that's how
we got our water till...

00:13:57.800 --> 00:14:01.290
Well, we got a well drilled
probably when I was 12 year old

00:14:01.290 --> 00:14:03.440
or probably 10, but...

00:14:03.440 --> 00:14:05.020
And we was probably one of the first ones

00:14:05.020 --> 00:14:07.400
to even have a drilled well in the holler

00:14:07.400 --> 00:14:09.230
'cause they didn't have electricity

00:14:09.230 --> 00:14:11.367
till probably the late 50s.

00:14:12.340 --> 00:14:15.331
And the man lived on Rock House is...

00:14:15.331 --> 00:14:17.680
He was the one put a
road through that holler.

00:14:17.680 --> 00:14:19.360
So, you know, they didn't have no road.

00:14:19.360 --> 00:14:23.293
They traveled the creek beds
and these old mountain roads.

00:14:24.730 --> 00:14:26.130
My mammaw's buried right
in the middle of one

00:14:26.130 --> 00:14:28.973
of them old mountain roads right now.

00:14:30.570 --> 00:14:32.520
- That's a very special
part of the country.

00:14:32.520 --> 00:14:34.453
- Oh yeah. It's real special.

00:14:36.550 --> 00:14:38.830
I'd like to go show you where I was raised

00:14:38.830 --> 00:14:39.663
and see how...

00:14:39.663 --> 00:14:40.940
You talk about beautiful

00:14:40.940 --> 00:14:42.830
but it is beautiful up in there.

00:14:42.830 --> 00:14:43.663
- Yeah.

00:14:43.663 --> 00:14:44.496
- It's got the falls.

00:14:44.496 --> 00:14:47.480
I mean it's, it's so beautiful.

00:14:47.480 --> 00:14:49.430
Everybody thinks it's beautiful.

00:14:49.430 --> 00:14:52.720
I mean, it is, and we would...

00:14:52.720 --> 00:14:56.338
Oh, when it snowed, oh, that was...

00:14:56.338 --> 00:14:58.410
We didn't care if it
stayed all year round then

00:14:58.410 --> 00:15:01.330
'cause you didn't have
no school, you know?

00:15:01.330 --> 00:15:04.550
And we would get out at
daylight on them ole sleds

00:15:04.550 --> 00:15:08.273
and it would get so
cold, we'd build fires up

00:15:08.273 --> 00:15:13.273
and warm our feet up by the fire.

00:15:14.070 --> 00:15:16.220
- What's your favorite
memory in your life?

00:15:20.240 --> 00:15:22.883
- My grandpa, I guess and my grandma.

00:15:24.450 --> 00:15:26.810
They was raised...

00:15:26.810 --> 00:15:29.840
They didn't have electric.

00:15:29.840 --> 00:15:34.840
She raised 12 kids by
the sweat of her brow.

00:15:35.710 --> 00:15:40.710
She growed everything they
eat, put it away in the fall.

00:15:41.390 --> 00:15:44.000
She had a milk cow that
she got up every morning

00:15:44.000 --> 00:15:47.300
at five o'clock and probably walked a mile

00:15:47.300 --> 00:15:49.380
just to milk the cow.

00:15:49.380 --> 00:15:51.130
And she done that twice a day

00:15:52.285 --> 00:15:55.047
'cause pappaw worked making mining props

00:15:57.010 --> 00:16:01.120
and they didn't get paid
probably 50 cents a week.

00:16:01.120 --> 00:16:02.540
You know what I'm saying?

00:16:02.540 --> 00:16:04.640
He didn't get nothing hardly

00:16:04.640 --> 00:16:07.520
and the only thing they
got out of the store

00:16:07.520 --> 00:16:09.273
would be like sugar,

00:16:10.263 --> 00:16:14.650
'cause she made her own
grease; her own lard.

00:16:14.650 --> 00:16:16.860
Well, they raised hogs, killed the hogs.

00:16:16.860 --> 00:16:19.470
Every winter, they would take turns,

00:16:19.470 --> 00:16:21.580
like two or three different families

00:16:21.580 --> 00:16:23.240
would have a hog to kill.

00:16:23.240 --> 00:16:26.520
This one would go up with
that one and kill their hog.

00:16:26.520 --> 00:16:27.930
Then the other one
would help the other one

00:16:27.930 --> 00:16:29.997
and they'd all share the meat

00:16:31.065 --> 00:16:32.183
and had smoke houses.

00:16:33.490 --> 00:16:34.820
I'd like for...

00:16:34.820 --> 00:16:36.410
Some people's doing that right now

00:16:36.410 --> 00:16:38.340
like living off the grid.

00:16:38.340 --> 00:16:40.450
I'd really like to do that.

00:16:40.450 --> 00:16:42.410
I mean, when I was little,

00:16:42.410 --> 00:16:45.290
I was little then but
I remember how it was.

00:16:45.290 --> 00:16:47.293
I'd follow him to milk the cows.

00:16:48.680 --> 00:16:52.160
Now I won't drink cow milk
probably just off the tit,

00:16:52.160 --> 00:16:53.770
you know what I'm saying?

00:16:53.770 --> 00:16:56.683
But then, that's all we had to drink.

00:16:58.627 --> 00:16:59.943
- Do you have any regrets?

00:17:01.430 --> 00:17:03.590
- Yeah, I regret quitting school. Yeah.

00:17:03.590 --> 00:17:05.150
- Yeah.

00:17:05.150 --> 00:17:06.473
What's the most important lesson

00:17:06.473 --> 00:17:08.806
you've learned in your life?

00:17:11.450 --> 00:17:16.050
- Well, you can get in
trouble real easy so...

00:17:17.880 --> 00:17:19.430
I learned my lesson, but

00:17:22.580 --> 00:17:24.033
I really can't explain it.

00:17:26.730 --> 00:17:28.300
Trying to, I mean...

00:17:29.880 --> 00:17:34.880
A lesson learned is like
getting out with your friends

00:17:34.960 --> 00:17:38.400
that you think is your big
buddies that can get you

00:17:38.400 --> 00:17:41.330
to do stuff you wouldn't normally do.

00:17:41.330 --> 00:17:45.570
And then, my lesson learned is

00:17:45.570 --> 00:17:49.920
they ain't always your
friends, that try...

00:17:49.920 --> 00:17:51.040
They're not your friend

00:17:51.040 --> 00:17:55.290
that tries to get you
to do stuff like that.

00:17:55.290 --> 00:17:58.864
Do good in life. Go for what's right.

00:17:58.864 --> 00:17:59.697
And be good.

00:18:01.100 --> 00:18:03.850
- All right. Sue, thank you so
much for sharing your story.

00:18:03.850 --> 00:18:04.683
- You're welcome.

00:18:04.683 --> 00:18:06.498
- Good luck. Good luck in mountains.

00:18:06.498 --> 00:18:07.430
(Sue laughing)

00:18:07.430 --> 00:18:08.770
- You'll have to go with us some time.

00:18:08.770 --> 00:18:10.120
- I'd like that. Thank you.

00:18:13.450 --> 00:18:14.860
- Yeah, you give me your number

00:18:14.860 --> 00:18:17.520
and I'll find you some good'ens.

